{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CghoUrVQugoVo3gZoADNz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Dq ≈ 1040 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 820 cm⁻¹**\n\n**Assignment.** The complex is high-spin d⁷ (Co(II)-like), with a ⁴T₁g(F) ground state. The three spin-allowed bands are:\n- ν₁ = ⁴T₁g(F) → ⁴T₂g(F) = 9 180 cm⁻¹\n- ν₂ = ⁴T₁g(F) → ⁴A₂g(F) = 19 550 cm⁻¹\n- ν₃ = ⁴T₁g(F) → ⁴T₁g(P) = 20 320 cm⁻¹\n\n**Energy expressions.** The ⁴F term splits as ⁴T₁g = −6Dq, ⁴T₂g = +2Dq and ⁴A₂g = +12Dq. The two ⁴T₁g levels, F and P, mix through a 2×2 matrix. I used the matrix [[−6Dq, 4Dq], [4Dq, 15B]] from memory. I checked it only in the strong-field limit, where it gives the t₂² energy of −8Dq, so the matrix is not fully verified. Its lower root E₋ is the ground state and its upper root E₊ is the ⁴T₁g(P) level.\n\n- ν₁ = 2Dq − E₋\n- ν₂ = 12Dq − E₋\n- ν₃ = E₊ − E₋\n\n**Solving.**\n1. ν₂ − ν₁ = 10Dq = 10 370 cm⁻¹, so Dq = 1 037 cm⁻¹.\n2. From ν₁, E₋ = 2Dq − 9 180 = −7 106 cm⁻¹.\n3. E₋ must satisfy (−6Dq − E₋)(15B − E₋) = 16Dq². With Dq = 1 037 this gives 884 × (15B + 7 106) = 1.72 × 10⁷, so 15B = 12 358 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 824 cm⁻¹.\n4. Check on the third band: E₊ = (−6Dq + 15B) − E₋ = 13 242 cm⁻¹, so ν₃ = E₊ − E₋ ≈ 20 350 cm⁻¹. This agrees with the observed 20 320 cm⁻¹ to within about 30 cm⁻¹.\n\nThe fit therefore uses all three bands consistently. B ≈ 824 cm⁻¹ is about 85% of the free-ion value of roughly 970 cm⁻¹, which is reasonable for Co(II).\n\n{\"Dq\": 1037, \"B\": 824, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"High-spin d7 Oh fit of ν1, ν2 and ν3 using the ⁴T₁g(F)–⁴T₁g(P) mixing gives Dq≈1037 and B≈824 cm⁻¹; C is not determined by spin-allowed bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1659,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1659,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_ti